      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one apply for SCSEP while receiving Social Security or retirement benefits?",
          "answer": "Yes, as long as their total household income remains at or below 125% of the federal poverty level. Social Security and pension income count toward the income limit, but receiving benefits doesn't disqualify them from SCSEP if they're still under the threshold and seeking work."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if there's no immediate opening but my loved one is eligible?",
          "answer": "Waitlists are common due to funding limits. Priority applicants (veterans, 65+, disabled, rural residents) get placed first when slots open. Your loved one's name goes on the waiting list and the local grantee will contact them when funding becomes available."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one choose what type of job training they receive?",
          "answer": "Training opportunities depend on available host sites in your area and your loved one's interests and abilities. Common placements include teacher's aide, administrative support, maintenance, customer service, and healthcare support roles. The local grantee works to match participants with suitable positions."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if my loved one finds a permanent job before completing the 6-month training?",
          "answer": "That's the goal of the program. Participants are expected to leave SCSEP when they find unsubsidized employment. The program provides job placement assistance and continues to support participants transitioning to permanent work."
        },
        {
          "question": "Does my veteran father automatically get accepted into SCSEP?",
          "answer": "Veterans receive priority, but they still must meet all eligibility requirements including the income limit and unemployment status. Priority means they're placed ahead of non-veterans when slots are available, but funding limits can still create waits."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one apply to multiple SCSEP grantees to increase their chances?",
          "answer": "No, participants can only be enrolled with one grantee at a time, and grantees typically serve specific geographic areas. Contact the grantee serving your county rather than applying to multiple organizations."
        },
        {
          "question": "How long can my loved one stay in the SCSEP program?",
          "answer": "The program is designed as temporary training, typically 6 months, with the goal of transitioning to unsubsidized employment. Extensions may be possible in limited circumstances, but the focus is on moving participants into permanent jobs as quickly as possible."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one has been out of work for many years?",
          "answer": "Long-term unemployment actually makes your loved one a priority candidate under the 'low employment prospects' category. The program specifically targets people who have had difficulty finding work and provides both skills training and confidence-building through real work experience."
        }
      ],
